PLACES=[
N('sainte-marie-church-church-point','Sainte-Marie Church','Church Point','ns','worship','standing',44.3332,-66.1173,'exact','view','drive','','1903–1905',
  'Finished in 1905 to the plans of the French architect Auguste Regneault, with the local master carpenter Léo Melanson overseeing the work, Sainte-Marie is the third church on this site for the Acadian families who settled Church Point after 1769. The Register says it is thought to be the largest wooden building in North America. Melanson was given a medal by Pope Pius XII in 1944 for his work on it.',
  'A French Romanesque Revival church, all wood, beside the campus of the Université Sainte-Anne. The spire is 185 feet high and can be seen from miles around; two large turrets flank the steeple and four small ones sit at its base. The Register says the inside and outside are relatively unaltered: rib-vaulted ceilings, a large nave and transepts, round pillars with Corinthian capitals, stained glass, and a crystal chandelier from the 1828 church.',
  'The largest wooden church in North America, built by Acadian carpenters.',
  'Parish church; the Register records the church and its land as a provincially designated property. Address 1713 Highway 1. Visiting hours and services not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Sainte Marie Church',5390)],brick='no',bcol='none',arch='Auguste Regneault',builder='Léo Melanson (master carpenter)',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
N('trinity-anglican-digby','Trinity Anglican Church','Digby','ns','worship','standing',44.62002,-65.75692,'site','view','drive','','1878–1880',
  'The second Trinity Church in Digby, a Loyalist town: the cornerstone was laid July 3, 1878 and Bishop Binney consecrated it on October 13, 1880. The architect was the American Stephen Earle, who designed over forty churches mostly in the Romanesque Revival manner, and the builder was M. L. Oliver of Digby. A parish hall of 1904 stands beside it.',
  'A wooden Gothic Revival church on a hillside at Mount and Queen streets above the waterfront, with board-and-batten at the base, pointed windows, clerestory windows and a spire set in an unusual place. The rose window of stained glass, with a Star of David in its frame, is by the London artist Alexander Gibbs. The cemetery is on the same property. The Register says it holds regular services; times not checked.',
  'Stephen Earle\'s wooden Gothic Revival church over Digby harbour.',
  'Anglican parish property; the church, hall, cemetery and land are provincially designated. Service times and interior access not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Trinity Anglican Church National Historic Site of Canada',7266)],brick='no',bcol='none',arch='Stephen Earle',builder='M. L. Oliver',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
N('old-meeting-house-barrington','Old Meeting House, Barrington','Barrington','ns','worship','standing',43.5666,-65.5797,'exact','open','drive','','1760s–1841',
  'Settlers from Cape Cod and Nantucket founded Barrington about 1761 and raised money from about 1765 to build a meeting house for worship and town business. Building began about 1771, the weather-tight shell went slowly, and the pews and interior were not finished until 1841. The Register calls it one of the oldest non-conformist houses of worship in Canada and the only survivor of five Congregationalist meeting houses built in Nova Scotia. Baptists, Methodists, Presbyterians and Anglicans all used it before each built their own church. It is now a Nova Scotia Museum site.',
  'A two-storey wooden building with a steep gable roof and central entrance, in the New England meeting-house style, in a small graveyard whose earliest stone is dated 1766 (Lettice Doane). The Register records that the old court house of 1841 stands across the street.',
  'A 1700s New England meeting house on the Nova Scotia shore, with a graveyard from the first settlers.',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum; admission by donation; closed Canada Day. Address 2408 Highway 3. Hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 2026 (the 2026 season). '+PARK,
  [HP('Old Meeting House National Historic Site of Canada',1263),L('Nova Scotia Museum: Old Meeting House, visit','https://meetinghouse.novascotia.ca/visit')],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='June 2 to September 26, Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (2026 season); closed Canada Day'),
N('ross-thomson-house-shelburne','Ross-Thomson House and Store','Shelburne','ns','civic','standing',43.761,-65.3238,'exact','open','drive','','1785–1786',
  'Brothers George and Robert Ross, Loyalists, built the house and store in 1785 to 1786 and traded overseas from it. The Register says it is the only remaining 18th-century store in Nova Scotia. A former clerk, Robert Thomson, bought the store in 1815 and his son kept it until 1880; it held the post office, and the room above served as a militia room during the Fenian raids of the late 1860s. It is now a museum.',
  'A large wooden building with its narrow end to the street, gable roof over the store at the south end and a gambrel roof at the north, standing on a granite foundation. Register details: heavy studded plank doors, windows flashed with birch bark on the north and west sides, a fireplace in the store with old mouldings, and a mail slot in a shutter.',
  'The only 18th-century store left in Nova Scotia, still a shop-and-house museum.',
  'Run as a museum, part of the Shelburne Historical Society\'s group (combined admission reported). Address 9 Charlotte Lane. Seasonal hours: search results quoting the museum\'s pages gave conflicting dates, so they are not given; check before a visit. '+PARK,
  [HP('Ross-Thomson House',1395)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='?'),
N('dory-shop-shelburne','The Dory Shop','Shelburne','ns','industry','standing',43.7608,-65.3241,'exact','open','drive','','1883–',
  'Built in 1883 by John Williams, this was one of seven dory shops in Shelburne at the height of the Grand Banks fishery. Shelburne dories were known from 1860 and became famous after Isaac Crowell patented his clip for joining the floor futtocks to the sides in 1887. The Register calls it the last dory shop left in the town; the Province bought it in 1979 and it is part of the Nova Scotia Museum.',
  'A long wooden shingle-clad shop of two and a half storeys on Shelburne Harbour, with barn-style doors, six-over-six windows and shutters. The Register says the original interior elements for dory building survive, including wooden floors and framing.',
  'The last working-style dory shop in the town that made the Grand Banks dory.',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum; adult admission $5, student or senior $4 (from the museum\'s page). Address 11 Dock Street. Hours from the museum\'s own page, read November 2026; some closed dates in 2026. '+PARK,
  [HP('The Dory Shop',4103),L('Nova Scotia Museum: Dory Shop, visit','https://doryshop.novascotia.ca/visit')],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='May 5 to September 26, Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. (2026 season)'),
N('barrington-woolen-mill','Barrington Woolen Mill','Barrington Head','ns','industry','standing',43.5677,-65.5784,'exact','open','drive','','1882–1962',
  'Shares sold around Barrington and Cape Sable Island paid for this mill, built in 1882 on the Barrington River to spin local fleece; it was running by 1884. Robert Doane leased it in 1894, then bought it and gave it its permanent name. The Register says it is one of only a few water-powered woolen mills left in eastern Canada and the only one on the east coast powered by a horizontal water wheel. It ran until 1962 and joined the Nova Scotia Museum in 1966.',
  'A red-shingled industrial building beside the river with its sluiceway, flume and dam. Most of the original machinery is inside and, the Register says, is in working order except the skeiner, including water-powered carding machines and a Knowles and Crompton loom; the water wheel still works. Floorboards and beams are soaked with lanolin.',
  'A water-powered woolen mill that kept its machines.',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum. Address 2368 Highway 3. Hours: from a search result quoting the museum\'s own site (June 1 to September 30, Tuesday to Saturday, 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.); the page itself could not be read and a listing elsewhere differs, so check before a visit. '+PARK,
  [HP('The Barrington Woolen Mill',8850)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='June 1 to September 30, Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (not confirmed for 2026)'),
N('tidnish-dock-ship-railway','Tidnish Dock (Chignecto Marine Transport Railway)','Tidnish','ns','rail','traces',45.9978,-64.0079,'site','public','walk','','1888–1891',
  'Tidnish Dock is the northern end of H. C. G. Ketchum\'s Chignecto Marine Transport Railway, a plan to lift ships out of the water onto a cradle and haul them across the Isthmus of Chignecto behind two locomotives. Work began in 1888 and the Register lists the dock, the Fort Lawrence terminus and the Tidnish Bridge site as the remaining evidence. The dock lies in a provincial park.',
  'The Register lists rock structures from the terminal, wooden pilings and the slipway that carried ships out of the water, seen at low tide, with a view of the Northumberland Strait. Where the pin lies exactly on the shore was not checked on the ground.',
  'Pilings and stonework of a ship railway that was never finished.',
  'Tidnish Dock Provincial Park, Province of Nova Scotia, off Route 366 at Tidnish Cross Roads. The remains show at low tide; mind the tide. Parking and park hours not checked.',
  [HP('Tidnish Dock Site',7830)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement'),
N('pictou-iron-foundry','Pictou Iron Foundry','Pictou','ns','industry','standing',45.6769,-62.7049,'exact','view','walk','','1855–',
  'William Henry Davies, a Staffordshire man who had managed the Albion Iron Foundry at Stellarton, set up this foundry on the Pictou waterfront in 1855. His sons ran it, then a company, then by the 1930s the Ferguson brothers, with about thirty men; in and after the Second World War it worked almost wholly on shipbuilding. The Register calls the three brick buildings the best known pre-National Policy industrial buildings in Nova Scotia and possibly the earliest surviving foundry buildings in the province.',
  'Three brick buildings round a shared courtyard on Front Street: two one-storey and one of two and a half storeys, with king-post roof trusses, six-over-six windows under inlaid brick arches, and a cut-stone foundation under the blacksmith\'s shop, which the Register says holds a remarkable collection of industrial objects. They are used for storage and apartments, so look from the street.',
  'The oldest iron foundry buildings in Nova Scotia, on the Pictou waterfront.',
  'Privately used (storage and apartments, per the Register); look from Front Street. '+PARK,
  [HP('Pictou Iron Foundry',14796)],brick='?',archw='no architect named in the Register statement'),
N('orangedale-railway-station','Orangedale Railway Station','Orangedale','ns','rail','standing',45.9005,-61.09268,'exact','open','drive','','1886–',
  'Built in 1886 on the Intercolonial Railway line between Port Hawkesbury and Sydney, this is the last wooden Intercolonial station in Nova Scotia and the oldest surviving station in the province, according to the Register. The Orangedale Railway Association restored it and keeps it as a museum with a caboose, an engine, freight cars and a snowplough.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey wooden station in "Railway Queen Anne" style: a steep cross-hipped roof with a raised centre section like a turret, eight long dormers, a bracketed soffit and the original awning roof over the platform, with the ticket office projecting below. The Register says it has remained virtually intact. It occasionally hosts a tourist excursion train.',
  'The oldest railway station left in Nova Scotia, kept by volunteers with its train.',
  'Run by the Orangedale Railway Association as a museum. Address 1428 Orangedale Road. Opening times: the only dated schedule found was for 2018 (daily in summer), so none is given; telephone 902-756-3384 per that page. '+PARK,
  [HP('Orangedale Railway Station',11800),L('Nova Scotia Railway Heritage: Orangedale','https://novascotiarailwayheritage.com/orangedale/')],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='?'),
N('lenoir-forge-arichat','LeNoir Forge','Arichat','ns','industry','standing',45.5103,-61.0116,'exact','open','drive','','1793–',
  'Thomas LeNoir and his brother, blacksmiths, supplied the shipyards of Arichat, the fourth busiest port in Nova Scotia between 1830 and 1870. Business at the forge had stopped by the end of the 19th century and the building decayed; after the municipality bought it, it was heavily rebuilt as a museum, with some walls reconstructed and a new roof.',
  'A one-and-a-half-storey stone building at the water\'s edge on the north shore of Arichat Harbour: rubble stone on the north and west walls, cut stone on the east and south, with sandstone corner quoins. Some of what you see is a reconstruction. Inside displays not checked.',
  'A stone blacksmith shop from the age of Arichat\'s shipyards.',
  'Municipal museum per the Register. Address 712 Lower Road. Opening times not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('LeNoir Forge',2806)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='?'),
N('saint-pierre-cheticamp','Paroisse Saint-Pierre','Chéticamp','ns','worship','standing',46.6246,-61.0157,'exact','view','drive','','1893–',
  'Father Pierre Fiset had this sandstone church built on the harbour edge in 1893, once a dug channel let larger boats into the harbour. It was designed by David Ouellet, a Quebec City architect of over fifty churches, and built by Hubert Morin of Trois-Pistoles with craftsmen from Quebec; parishioners quarried the stone on Chéticamp Island. Father Fiset is buried beneath it.',
  'A Neo-Classical sandstone church whose 165-foot west tower, with a rose window over the door, ends the Cabot Trail at its western end. A second steeple sits over the chancel; the apse and chapel are at the east end. The Register calls the interior the most successful part of Ouellet\'s design; interior and visiting times not checked.',
  'A big stone Acadian church at the harbour, with a tower seen along the Cabot Trail.',
  'Parish church; the building and land are provincially designated. Service times not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Paroisse Saint-Pierre',7257)],brick='no',bcol='none',arch='David Ouellet',builder='Hubert Morin',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
N('prescott-house-starrs-point','Prescott House Museum (Acacia Grove)','Starr\'s Point','ns','landmark','standing',45.11059,-64.37917,'site','open','drive','','1809–1818',
  'Charles Ramage Prescott, a Halifax merchant and legislator who brought many fruit and fodder plants to Nova Scotia and is credited with introducing the Gravenstein apple to the Maritimes, built this house about 1809 to 1818 and lived in it until his death in 1859. His family kept it until 1971, when it joined the Nova Scotia Museum; his great-granddaughter had restored the house and gardens in the 1930s.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey Georgian house of light red local brick on a rubble-stone basement fronted with Wallace sandstone, with a five-bay front and a truncated hip roof, on a 32-acre site with gardens. One of the black locust ("acacia") trees Prescott planted remains.',
  'A Georgian brick house and the gardens of Nova Scotia\'s apple pioneer.',
  'Run by the Nova Scotia Museum. Address 1633 Starr\'s Point Road. The last dated hours found were for 2025 (June 1 to October 4, Wednesday to Sunday, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.); 2026 not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Prescott House',1499)],brick='yes',bcol='red',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='?'),
N('yarmouth-county-museum','Yarmouth County Museum','Yarmouth','ns','civic','standing',43.8368,-66.1161,'exact','open','walk','','1892–1893',
  'Built as Tabernacle Congregational Church after lightning burned the wooden church on March 12, 1892; the cornerstone was laid that August and it was dedicated on August 20, 1893. The plans were by Stephenson and Greene of New York and the stonework by Melford and William Sims, their only granite building. After the churches merged it became the Central United Church; in 1967 the Yarmouth County Historical Society bought it for its collections, and it has been the museum since 1969.',
  'A Gothic Revival church of rough Shelburne granite, one of only two granite buildings in Yarmouth, in the Collins Heritage Conservation District, with an asymmetrical front close to the street. It is now a museum of county history.',
  'A granite church turned county museum.',
  'Run by the Yarmouth County Historical Society. Address 22 Collins Street. Opening times not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Yarmouth County Museum',6519)],brick='no',bcol='none',arch='Stephenson & Greene',builder='Melford and William Sims (stonework)',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
N('wallace-river-swing-bridge','Wallace River Railway Swing Bridge','Wallace River','ns','rail','standing',45.7924,-63.5325,'site','view','hike','','1880s',
  'Built after the 1883 Nova Scotia government line between Truro and Pictou, with a swing span so ships could reach the sandstone quarries and loading piers at Wallace; the quarries shipped stone to Boston and New York. The Register says few swing bridges remain in Nova Scotia and none is as original or well kept. Iron parts were reportedly from the Dominion Bridge Company of Montreal. The swing span no longer works and the deck was replaced for walkers.',
  'A bridge of three plate girders, a through truss and a fixed swing span turning on a sandstone pier, with sandstone abutments and an iron trestle at the west end, high over a bend in the Wallace River in dense forest, 2 km upstream of Route 6. It is on the Trans Canada Trail. The Register says quarry workings and wharf piles can also be found.',
  'A rare swing railway bridge in the woods above a vanished quarry port.',
  'On the Trans Canada Trail; land owner not stated in the Register statement. The pin marks the Register\'s point and was checked only on the aerial. Parking not checked.',
  [HP('Wallace River Railway Swing Bridge',6941)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement'),
N('old-guysborough-court-house','Old Guysborough Court House Museum','Guysborough','ns','civic','standing',45.3892,-61.4986,'exact','open','drive','','1842–1843',
  'Designed and built by the carpenter Elisha Randall in 1842 to 1843 and the main public building of a county over 250 miles from Halifax, it held the Court of General Sessions and later the council. In 1867 voters here chose two anti-Confederation candidates. It stopped being a court house in 1973 and is a museum run by the Guysborough Historical Society.',
  'A plain, symmetrical wooden meeting-house form with shingles, a street-side porch and a side wing, set apart by its pointed Gothic windows. The Register lists the original court rooms still there: the judge\'s bench, gallery, barristers\' room, jury rooms and spectators\' benches.',
  'A county court house of 1843 with its judge\'s bench still in place.',
  'Museum of the Guysborough Historical Society. Address 283 Church Street. Opening times not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Old Guysborough Court House Museum',3053)],brick='no',bcol='none',arch='Elisha Randall',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
N('saint-ninians-cathedral-antigonish','Saint Ninian\'s Cathedral','Antigonish','ns','worship','standing',45.62,-61.9935,'exact','view','drive','','1867–1874',
  'Bishop Colin MacKinnon, who had moved St. Francis Xavier University to Antigonish, laid the cornerstone on June 29, 1867 and the cathedral was dedicated on September 13, 1874; the Register gives the building span as 1867 to 1886. The Diocese of Antigonish was set up in 1886. It stands on the edge of the university campus.',
  'A large nine-bay Romanesque Revival stone church of local limestone with sandstone trim and two square towers 125 feet high; carved crests of Bishop MacKinnon and Pope Pius IX flank the door under the Gaelic words "Tigh Dhe" (house of God). The Register describes frescoes on the ceiling, colonnades down the nave, Romanesque arches with standing figures and etched glass. Visiting hours not checked.',
  'A stone Romanesque cathedral with painted ceiling, on the university campus.',
  'Cathedral of the Diocese of Antigonish; the building and land are designated. Address 121 Saint Ninian\'s Street. Visiting hours not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Saint Ninian\'s Cathedral',4600)],brick='no',bcol='none',archw='no architect named in the Register statement',hours='?'),
N('st-peters-canal','St. Peter\'s Canal','St. Peter\'s','ns','canal','standing',45.65488,-60.86904,'site','view','walk','','1854–1869',
  'A national historic site, this canal links the Bras d\'Or Lakes to St. Peter\'s Bay on the Atlantic. It was built in phases from 1854 to 1869 and enlarged twice, and has carried commercial cargo and later pleasure boats.',
  'The Register lists the 800-metre passage cut through a granite hill, the walls, the first lock chamber and lock, piles of spoil on the lake and ocean shores, the lockmaster\'s residence and a movable swing bridge, all part of the historic site.',
  'A canal cut through a granite hill between lake and ocean.',
  'National historic site on Highway 4 in St. Peter\'s. Operating manager and visiting hours not checked in this session. '+PARK,
  [HP('St. Peters Canal National Historic Site of Canada',4487)],brick='?',archw='no architect named in the Register statement'),
N('holy-trinity-anglican-yarmouth','Holy Trinity Anglican Church, Yarmouth','Yarmouth','ns','worship','standing',43.8326,-66.1175,'exact','view','walk','','1866–1870',
  'A large brick Gothic Revival church of 1866 to 1870 designed by the Rev. J. R. Roy Campbell, a priest trained as an architect in England, and built by Baker, Bridgeo and Brown. It stands near the first Anglican church in Yarmouth of about 1793; the bell of 1835 and the marble font from the earlier church were moved into it.',
  'A cruciform brick church with freestone trim, a castellated tower at the southwest corner and stained glass windows. A panelled door from the 1793 church is kept in the parish hall. Interior and service times not checked.',
  'A brick Gothic Revival parish church, with furnishings from its 1800s predecessor.',
  'Anglican parish property; municipal heritage designation covers the building and land. Address 63 William Street. Service times not checked. '+PARK,
  [HP('Holy Trinity Anglican Church',7047)],brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='J. R. Roy Campbell',builder='Baker, Bridgeo & Brown',archw='Canadian Register of Historic Places statement',hours='?'),
]
